    "en": {
      "stem": "A 65-year-old patient with a large pleural effusion undergoes thoracentesis. The physician inserts a needle into the lower part of the thorax to aspirate fluid accumulated in the lowest part of the pleural cavity. The needle is inserted immediately superior to the upper border of a rib. Which of the following best explains the proper positioning of the needle?",
      "options": {
        "a": "Fluid accumulates in the costodiaphragmatic recess, and the main intercostal neurovascular bundle runs in the costal groove along the inferior border of the rib",
        "b": "Fluid accumulates in the costomediastinal recess, and the main intercostal neurovascular bundle runs along the superior border of the rib",
        "c": "Fluid accumulates between the visceral pleura and the lung parenchyma, and the main intercostal neurovascular bundle runs within the pleural cavity",
        "d": "Fluid accumulates in the mediastinum, and the main intercostal neurovascular bundle runs in the costal groove along the superior border of the rib",
        "e": "Fluid accumulates between the parietal pleura and endothoracic fascia, and the main intercostal neurovascular bundle runs deep to the parietal pleura"
      },
      "correct": "a",
      "explanation": "In a pleural effusion, fluid accumulates predominantly in the most dependent part of the pleural cavity, which is the costodiaphragmatic recess. Thoracentesis therefore targets this region. The main intercostal neurovascular bundle, consisting of the intercostal vein, artery, and nerve, runs within the costal groove along the inferior border of the rib. Inserting the needle immediately superior to the upper border of the rib therefore reduces the risk of injuring the main neurovascular bundle."
    }
