      "stem": "A 62-year-old man suffers an ischemic stroke involving the right side of the thalamus. After the acute phase, he has impaired perception of pain, temperature, fine touch, and proprioception on the left side of the body, while facial sensation is relatively preserved. Which thalamic nucleus was most likely affected?",
      "options": {
        "a": "Ventral posterolateral nucleus",
        "b": "Ventral posteromedial nucleus",
        "c": "Ventral anterior nucleus",
        "d": "Mediodorsal nucleus",
        "e": "Lateral posterior nucleus"
      },
      "correct": "a",
      "explanation": "The ventral posterolateral nucleus (VPL) receives somatosensory information from the contralateral side of the body, particularly through the medial lemniscus and spinothalamic tract, and subsequently transmits this information via thalamocortical projections to the primary somatosensory cortex in the postcentral gyrus. A lesion of the right VPL therefore causes impaired somatosensory perception on the left side of the body. The ventral posteromedial nucleus (VPM), in contrast, primarily processes somatosensory information from the face and also receives gustatory information. The ventral anterior nucleus is part of the motor circuitry and contributes to projections from the basal ganglia and cerebellum to motor cortical areas. The mediodorsal nucleus has important connections with the prefrontal cortex and limbic structures. The lateral posterior nucleus is part of the association thalamus and contributes to connections with parietal association areas."
