    "en": {
      "stem": "A patient with bilateral damage to the medial temporal lobe exhibits markedly reduced fear and has difficulty recognizing the emotional significance of facial expressions, particularly fearful expressions. Which structure was most likely damaged?",
      "options": {
        "a": "Amygdala",
        "b": "Hippocampus",
        "c": "Caudate nucleus",
        "d": "Thalamus",
        "e": "Hypothalamus"
      },
      "correct": "a",
      "explanation": "The amygdala is a nuclear complex located in the anterior medial temporal lobe near the uncus. It is involved in processing emotionally salient stimuli, particularly fear-related responses, and in forming emotional memories. Bilateral damage to the amygdala can result in reduced fear and impaired recognition of fearful facial expressions. The hippocampus is primarily involved in the consolidation of declarative memories and spatial navigation, the caudate nucleus contributes to motor and cognitive functions of the basal ganglia, the thalamus is involved in relaying and processing sensory information, and the hypothalamus regulates autonomic, endocrine, and homeostatic functions."
    }
