    "en": {
      "stem": "A patient has a lesion affecting the facial nerve nucleus in the caudal pons. Which of the following deficits would be expected as a result of this lesion?",
      "options": {
        "a": "Ipsilateral paralysis of the muscles of facial expression",
        "b": "Ipsilateral loss of taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue",
        "c": "Decreased secretion from the lacrimal gland",
        "d": "Loss of somatosensory sensation from the ipsilateral half of the face",
        "e": "Dysphagia due to impaired motor innervation of the muscles of the pharynx"
      },
      "correct": "a",
      "explanation": "The facial nerve nucleus is a somatomotor nucleus located in the caudal pons. It contains motor neurons whose axons travel through the facial nerve to innervate the muscles of facial expression. Therefore, a lesion of this nucleus causes ipsilateral paralysis of the muscles of facial expression. Taste afferent fibers of the facial nerve terminate in the nucleus of the solitary tract, parasympathetic fibers arise from the superior salivatory nucleus, somatosensory information from the face is primarily processed in the sensory nuclei of the trigeminal nerve, and the motor innervation of most pharyngeal muscles is primarily associated with the ambiguus nucleus."
    }
