    "en": {
      "stem": "A patient has a tumor in the cerebellopontine angle that compresses the vestibulocochlear nerve. Through which structure does this nerve pass as it travels from the cranial cavity to the inner ear?",
      "options": {
        "a": "Internal acoustic meatus",
        "b": "Facial canal",
        "c": "Jugular foramen",
        "d": "Carotid canal",
        "e": "Foramen ovale"
      },
      "correct": "a",
      "explanation": "The vestibulocochlear nerve enters the internal acoustic meatus from the cerebellopontine angle within the petrous part of the temporal bone. Together with the facial nerve and intermediate nerve, it passes through this canal toward the inner ear. The cochlear part of the vestibulocochlear nerve then continues toward the cochlea, whereas its vestibular part courses toward the vestibular apparatus. The facial canal contains the facial nerve, while the jugular foramen, carotid canal, and foramen ovale transmit other structures."
    }
