    "en": {
      "stem": "During histological examination of the kidney, a tubular system is identified that receives urine from the distal portions of several nephrons and then carries it toward the renal papilla. Which structure has been identified?",
      "options": {
        "a": "Collecting duct",
        "b": "Proximal convoluted tubule",
        "c": "Distal convoluted tubule",
        "d": "Thin limb of the loop of Henle",
        "e": "Glomerulus"
      },
      "correct": "a",
      "explanation": "Collecting ducts receive urine from the distal convoluted tubules of multiple nephrons and carry it toward the renal papilla. They are not part of the nephron but form the subsequent drainage system. The proximal convoluted tubule continues from the renal corpuscle and is the major site of reabsorption of substances from the filtrate. The distal convoluted tubule is part of the nephron and delivers urine to the collecting system. The thin limb of the loop of Henle contributes to the establishment of the osmotic gradient in the renal medulla. The glomerulus is part of the renal corpuscle and is involved in blood filtration."
    }
