      "explanation": "The middle transverse rectal fold (Kohlrausch fold) is the most prominent transverse fold of the rectum. It is located approximately 7 cm from the anal opening, typically on the right wall of the rectum, and is formed by thickening of the circular layer of the muscularis. The superior and inferior transverse rectal folds represent additional transverse folds of the rectum, but their location and clinical significance are less constant. The longitudinal rectal fold and anal column are not transverse folds formed by thickening of the rectal muscular layer."
