    "en": {
      "stem": "During urinary catheterization in a man, the penis is elevated to a horizontal position, straightening one of the two natural curvatures of the male urethra. Which curvature persists after this maneuver?",
      "options": {
        "a": "Infrapubic curvature",
        "b": "Prepubic curvature",
        "c": "Curvature at the navicular fossa",
        "d": "Curvature at the prostatic urethra",
        "e": "Curvature at the external urethral orifice"
      },
      "correct": "a",
      "explanation": "The infrapubic curvature of the male urethra is relatively fixed and is not straightened when the penis is elevated to a horizontal position. In contrast, the prepubic curvature is mobile and is straightened by this maneuver. The infrapubic curvature therefore remains present during proper positioning of the penis for catheterization. The navicular fossa is an expansion of the distal portion of the spongy urethra rather than a separate physiological curvature. The prostatic urethra and external urethral orifice also do not represent separate physiological curvatures of the urethra."
    }
