    "en": {
      "stem": "A patient with unilateral damage to a region of the pons develops weakness of biting, and the mandible deviates toward the affected side when the mouth is opened. Facial sensation is intact. Which structure is most likely affected?",
      "options": {
        "a": "Motor trigeminal nucleus",
        "b": "Principal sensory trigeminal nucleus",
        "c": "Spinal trigeminal nucleus",
        "d": "Mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus",
        "e": "Facial motor nucleus"
      },
      "correct": "a",
      "explanation": "The motor trigeminal nucleus is located in the pons and contains motor neurons whose axons join the mandibular nerve (V3). The motor fibers of the mandibular nerve supply primarily the masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid, and lateral pterygoid muscles, as well as the mylohyoid and anterior belly of the digastric muscle. With unilateral damage to the motor component of the trigeminal nerve, the mandible deviates toward the affected side when the mouth is opened because of the unopposed pull of the intact lateral pterygoid muscle. Preserved facial sensation is consistent with isolated involvement of the motor component."
    }
