    "en": {
      "stem": "A patient has damage to the gustatory afferent pathway of the facial nerve. Examination reveals loss of taste from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue on the affected side. Into which brainstem nucleus do the central processes of these afferent neurons normally enter?",
      "options": {
        "a": "Nucleus of the solitary tract",
        "b": "Facial nerve nucleus",
        "c": "Superior salivatory nucleus",
        "d": "Spinal trigeminal nucleus",
        "e": "Ambiguus nucleus"
      },
      "correct": "a",
      "explanation": "Taste afferent fibers from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue travel through the chorda tympani and subsequently through the intermediate nerve, which is functionally associated with the facial nerve. The central processes of these neurons enter the brainstem and terminate in the nucleus of the solitary tract, primarily in its rostral gustatory portion. The facial nerve nucleus contains somatomotor neurons for the muscles of facial expression. The superior salivatory nucleus contains preganglionic parasympathetic neurons. The spinal trigeminal nucleus primarily processes pain and temperature somatosensory information from the face. The ambiguus nucleus contains motor neurons that contribute to the innervation of the muscles of the pharynx and larynx."
    }
